# THE CAP, PLANTED BOTH WAYS, WITH THE TOKEN AND THE WALL TIME.
# 2026-10-07. Read the TOKEN, never rc: rc 3 is sh -c 'exit 3' at 0 MB and a kill at 2988 MB.
run	belt_rows_paid	rows_left_UNKNOWN	token	rc	wall_seconds	what it answers
uncapped (BEFORE the cap)	1641 (all)	0	TIMED-OUT	-9	>600	NOTHING. out=0B err=0B -- the report was never written.
uncapped, 07:16, prior unit	1641 (all)	0	WITHIN-LIMITS	0	524	THE WHOLE CENSUS, on a quiet tree. Same day, same file: TIMED-OUT AND WITHIN-LIMITS ARE BOTH TRUE.
--plant (synthetic tree)	n/a	n/a	WITHIN-LIMITS	0	1	every verdict on a 5-column fixture, armed AND spent
--belt-rows 20 (OVER-CAP)	20 of 20	1299 of 2087	WITHIN-LIMITS	0	562	the census, 20 rows belt-checked, 1299 published UNKNOWN
--belt-rows 400 (UNDER-CAP)	400 of 400	916 of 2084	WITHIN-LIMITS	0	428	the census, 400 rows belt-checked, 916 published UNKNOWN
# A SPENT BUDGET YIELDS `UNKNOWN needs=rerun-with-more-seconds`, NEVER `UNNAMED`.
# `UNNAMED` means EVERY TEST RAN, and a spent budget would make that a lie in the exact direction
# this file exists to prevent. --plant runs the fixture TWICE (armed, and BeltBudget(0,0) spent) and
# the spent pass asserts UNKNOWN, so the bound is asserted by the same oracle as what it changes.
#
# WALL TIMES ARE CONTENDED and are reported as an ORDER, not a millisecond: the 562 s run spent FEWER
# belt calls than the 428 s one. The trustworthy figures are the ROW COUNTS and the per-call cost
# (0.32 s), which do not depend on what else is running.